
<% content_for :title, "Social Pressure Relapse Plan | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="relapse-plan-page relapse-plan-social-pressure">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-hero">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <%= link_to "← ALL RELAPSE PLANS",
          toolbox_relapse_plans_path,
          class: "relapse-plan-back" %>

      <p class="relapse-plan-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / RELAPSE PLANS / SOCIAL PRESSURE
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>EVERYONE'S</span>
        <span>DRINKING.</span>
        <span>AND?</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="relapse-plan-intro">
        YOUR SOBRIETY PLAN FOR PARTIES,
        CELEBRATIONS &amp; "JUST ONE."
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / YOUR EXPERIENCE -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-pink">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        01 / YOUR EXPERIENCE
      </p>

      <h2>
        WHERE THE<br>
        PRESSURE LIVES.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          Being around alcohol can be complicated.
          You used to enjoy drinking. You liked
          the excitement, the relaxation, and
          the way a few drinks could make
          an ordinary gathering feel
          more entertaining.
        </p>

        <p>
          Now you're sober, but other people
          still drink. Birthdays, holidays,
          celebrations, dinners, and casual
          get-togethers haven't stopped
          including alcohol just because
          you've decided not to.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sometimes the pressure comes
          from someone else. They offer
          you a drink, question why
          you're not drinking, or
          insist that one won't hurt.
        </p>

        <p>
          Other times, nobody says
          anything. You see everyone
          having a good time, remember
          what drinking felt like,
          and start wishing you
          could participate the
          way you used to.
        </p>

        <p>
          You might feel left out,
          irritated, nostalgic,
          or tired of having to
          think about sobriety
          when everyone else
          seems free to enjoy
          themselves.
        </p>

        <p>
          The temptation isn't
          necessarily about
          wanting to escape.
          Sometimes you want
          to celebrate, relax,
          or have fun.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            You can miss social drinking
            without deciding to
            start drinking again.
            Other people's choices
            don't have to become yours.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / BEFORE YOU GO -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-black">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        02 / BEFORE YOU GO
      </p>

      <h2>
        PLAN THE<br>
        WHOLE NIGHT.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- THE SITUATION -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>THE SITUATION</h3>

          <p>
            Before agreeing to go,
            consider what kind of
            gathering you're attending.
            Is alcohol part of
            the occasion, or
            is drinking the
            entire activity?
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about who's
            going to be there,
            how much drinking
            you expect, and
            whether the environment
            has been difficult
            for you before.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check in with yourself.
            Are you feeling stable?
            Have you been sleeping?
            Are you already dealing
            with cravings, irritability,
            depression, or impulsivity?
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're noticing
            symptoms of mania,
            increased impulsivity,
            or strong drinking
            urges, reconsider
            whether attending
            is a good idea.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't have to
            attend every event
            you're invited to.
            Declining is an option,
            not a failure.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- SUPPORT PERSON -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR SUPPORT PERSON</h3>

          <p>
            Choose someone who
            knows you're sober
            and will respect
            that decision.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you're attending
            with a friend, let
            them know you may
            leave early or
            step away if
            drinking starts
            feeling tempting.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also arrange
            for someone outside
            the event to be
            available by text
            or phone.
          </p>

          <p>
            A simple message works:
            "I'm going somewhere
            people will be drinking.
            I'm fine going, but
            I might need to
            check in if I
            start getting tempted."
          </p>

          <p>
            Have a backup contact.
            If your first person
            is unavailable,
            you should still
            have someone to reach.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- PRACTICAL SETUP -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR PRACTICAL SETUP</h3>

          <p>
            Decide ahead of time
            that you're not
            drinking. Don't
            leave the decision
            open for negotiation
            once you're there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Know what you'll
            drink instead.
            Water, soda,
            sparkling water,
            or another
            nonalcoholic option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Eat beforehand
            or make sure
            food is available.
            Being hungry,
            tired, or
            uncomfortable
            can make an
            already tempting
            situation harder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider setting
            a time to leave.
            You can enjoy
            part of an event
            without staying
            until everyone
            is intoxicated.
          </p>

          <p>
            Don't make your
            sobriety depend
            on the hope
            that nobody
            offers you alcohol.
            Have your response
            ready before
            you need it.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- WAY HOME -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>YOUR WAY HOME</h3>

          <p>
            Decide how you're
            getting home
            before you go.
            Don't depend
            on someone who
            may be drinking
            to drive you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Arrange a sober
            driver, reliable
            transportation,
            or another safe
            way to leave
            independently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Have a backup
            if your original
            ride falls through.
            If necessary,
            take an Uber.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you've consumed
            alcohol, don't drive,
            even if you feel
            capable of doing so.
            Do not drive at all. You know what the stakes are.
          </p>

          <p>
            You should be able
            to leave when
            you want without
            having to negotiate
            with someone else.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              Your exit plan
              is part of
              your sobriety plan.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / IN THE MOMENT -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-yellow">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        03 / IN THE MOMENT
      </p>

      <h2>
        HAVE THE<br>
        WORDS READY.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-grid">

        <!-- SOMEONE OFFERS -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHEN SOMEONE OFFERS</h3>

          <p>
            You don't need to
            explain your sobriety
            every time someone
            offers you a drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            "No thanks,
            I'm good."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'm not drinking."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'll have a
            soda instead."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'm all set,
            thanks."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I can't drink."
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep it simple.
            You don't owe
            anyone your
            recovery history,
            medical information,
            or an explanation
            of your decision.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most offers can
            end with a
            straightforward no.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- SOMEONE PUSHES -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHEN SOMEONE PUSHES</h3>

          <p>
            If someone keeps
            insisting, you
            can be direct
            without turning
            it into an argument.
          </p>

          <p>
            "I said no.
            I'm not drinking."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'm not interested
            in discussing it."
          </p>

          <p>
            "Please stop
            offering me alcohol."
          </p>

          <p>
            "You can drink.
            I'm choosing not to."
          </p>

          <p>
            If the pressure
            continues, move
            away from that
            person or leave
            the event.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't have
            to convince
            anyone that your
            sobriety is
            reasonable.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              No is a complete
              sentence, even
              when someone
              doesn't like it.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- BARGAINING -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHEN YOU START BARGAINING</h3>

          <p>
            Pay attention when
            you start thinking:
            "Just one won't hurt,"
            "Everyone else is
            drinking," or
            "I've been sober
            long enough."
          </p>

          <p>
            Maybe you tell
            yourself you
            deserve to
            celebrate, or
            that this
            particular occasion
            should be an exception.
          </p>

          <p>
            Recognize that
            you're no longer
            simply attending
            a social event.
            You're starting
            to negotiate
            with your sobriety.
          </p>

          <p>
            Play the tape
            forward. Remember
            what you liked
            about drinking,
            then remember
            everything that
            came afterward.
            Remember jail?
          </p>

          <p>
            Step away from
            the alcohol,
            contact your
            support person,
            and consider
            leaving.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your mood
            is elevated,
            you're unusually
            impulsive, or
            your judgment
            feels different,
            don't treat
            that as a reason
            to make an exception.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              A special occasion
              doesn't erase
              your history
              with alcohol.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- LEAVING -->
        <div class="relapse-plan-box">

          <h3>WHEN YOU WANT TO LEAVE</h3>

          <p>
            You can leave
            whenever you
            decide the event
            is no longer
            working for you.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't need
            to wait until
            you're miserable,
            overwhelmed,
            or seriously
            considering drinking.
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'm heading out.
            Thanks for having me."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I'm tired,
            so I'm going
            to call it
            a night."
          </p>

          <p>
            "I've had fun,
            but I'm ready
            to go home."
          </p>

          <p>
            Use your planned
            transportation.
            If that isn't
            available, call a rideshare.
          </p>

          <p>
            You don't have
            to apologize
            for leaving
            before everyone
            else is finished.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>
              Leaving early
              is better than
              staying until
              you make a decision
              you regret.
            </strong>
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / AFTERWARD -->
  <section class="relapse-plan-section relapse-plan-purple">
    <div class="relapse-plan-container">

      <p class="relapse-plan-label">
        04 / AFTERWARD
      </p>

      <h2>
        THE NIGHT<br>
        DOESN'T END THERE.
      </h2>

      <div class="relapse-plan-copy">

        <p>
          Once you're home,
          check in with
          yourself. How
          did the evening
          actually go?
          Did you enjoy
          yourself? Feel
          uncomfortable?
          Experience cravings?
        </p>

        <p>
          Notice what
          worked. Maybe
          having a nonalcoholic
          drink made the
          situation easier.
          Maybe leaving
          early helped,
          or checking in
          with a friend
          interrupted
          a craving.
        </p>

        <p>
          Be equally honest
          about what didn't
          work. Were you
          more tempted than
          you expected?
          Did someone
          pressure you?
          Did you start
          making exceptions
          in your head?
        </p>

        <p>
          If you leave
          feeling restless,
          nostalgic, or
          disappointed,
          pay attention.
          Sometimes cravings
          continue after
          you've left
          the environment
          that triggered them.
        </p>

        <p>
          Don't stop at
          the liquor store
          on the way home
          or decide to
          drink alone
          because you
          resisted drinking
          at the event.
        </p>

        <p>
          Eat something,
          unwind, spend
          time with Banjo,
          watch a movie,
          or contact
          someone who
          supports your sobriety.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the craving
          continues or
          becomes stronger,
          reach out to
          a friend or someone from Harmony.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you drank,
          don't turn one
          decision into
          an entire weekend
          of drinking.
          Stop when you can,
          don't drive,
          contact support,
          and return to
          your relapse
          prevention plan.
        </p>

        <p>
          Review the evening
          later. You might
          decide that certain
          people, places,
          or situations
          aren't worth
          repeating right now.
          You might also
          discover that
          some gatherings
          are perfectly
          manageable sober.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>
            You don't have
            to stop celebrating,
            seeing friends,
            or participating
            in your life.
            You can enjoy
            the occasion
            without drinking.
          </strong>
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

</main>
